<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android 12+ (API 31+) backup scope. The pre-12 rules live in backup_rules.xml and MUST be
  kept in step with this file — minSdk is 24, so most phones this app is built for read that
  one, not this one.

  WHY THIS FILE CHANGED (Vishal, 2026-08-27)
  Until now everything here was excluded and allowBackup was false. The reason was sound at the
  time: the app held a learner's NAME, her 90-day auth token, her not-yet-synced wrong-answer
  text, and — on a shared campus laptop — a cached roster with OTHER children's names, PIN
  hashes and tokens. None of that may ride out of the device on the device owner's Google
  account, and the published data-deletion page promised parents it never would.

  The packaged Play build no longer holds ANY of it. Accounts are web-only now (see
  AUTH_ENABLED in index.html and the cut in bundle-game.js): there is no login, so no name, no
  email, no token and no roster; recordEvent()/recordAttempt() return early with no identity,
  so no wrong-answer text or attendance is ever written; and retireAccounts() erases whatever
  an earlier build left behind on the first launch after the update.

  What remains under app_webview is anonymous and nothing but: star counts per activity, a gem
  total, which module tests were passed, which questions have been seen, the UI settings, and a
  cache of the lesson content itself. That is the entire payload, and losing it is the ONLY
  thing standing between a child and starting the whole curriculum again after a reinstall —
  which, with no account to restore from, is otherwise permanent.

  ⚠ BEFORE ROLLING OUT A BUILD WITH THIS FILE: the data-deletion page at
  bodhya.net/data-deletion still tells parents that uninstalling the app removes the on-device
  copy. With Auto Backup on, a copy also sits in the device owner's own Google Drive and comes
  back on reinstall. That sentence has to say so first, or the app ships against its own
  published promise. ☰ → "My progress" → "Start fresh" clears the device copy, and the next
  backup cycle overwrites the Drive copy with the emptied state.

  Scope is app_webview and nothing else — the WebView origin store where all of the above
  lives. Naming it explicitly, rather than backing up the data directory wholesale, keeps any
  future file a plugin decides to write OUT of the backup until somebody adds it here on
  purpose.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <include domain="root" path="app_webview" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <include domain="root" path="app_webview" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
